+{"timestamp":"2026-08-28T12:12:00Z","branch":"fixed-center finite-state invariant","note":"For a convex quadrilateral ABCD, let O be the unique intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of diagonals AC and BD. If ABCE is a partner and E is the reflection of D across the perpendicular bisector l of AC, then O lies on l and is fixed by the reflection, so OE=OD. Since O also lies on the perpendicular bisector of BD, OD=OB, hence OE=OB; therefore O lies on the perpendicular bisector of BE. Together with O on the perpendicular bisector of AC and uniqueness for the convex quadrilateral ABCE, this proves the same O persists. This avoids the misleading shortcut that segment BD itself reflects to BE when B need not lie on l."}
